# Reproduction (FRICTION F19): run `kanso check bugs/param_name_leak/kit`
# (ok), then `kanso check bugs/param_name_leak/app`.
#
# Expected: ok. Inside kit, `keep?` is this function's parameter, and app's
# own top-level `keep?` has nothing to do with it.
# Actual: error[exhaustive]: this can be a none and `keep?` has no arm for
# it (in kit.kso) (module .../app), pointing at line 13 below. Checking kit
# on app's behalf resolves the parameter against app's function. Binding
# `xs[1]` to a name first makes the check pass, and the program then prints
# `kept true`, so the engines call the right function; only the checker
# confuses the two.
pub fn first_kept xs keep?
  if (keep? xs[1]) "kept" "dropped"
